n is normally enough: call it again only if the user says they changed the list, or a request was refused because its names did not match. It only reads; it connects nothing. A folder whose `online` is false cannot answer: do not request it. Tell the user that computer is not connected right now (Claude is not running there, or Remote Control is off), ask them to open it, then call this again.", "eager_input_streaming": true, "input_schema": {"properties": {}, "required": [], "type": "object"}, "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__list_computer_folders"}, "server": "claude-code-remote"}, {"description": "List Claude Code Remote environments for the current user. Returns environment IDs, names, kinds, and states. Use this to pick an environment_id for create_session.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__list_environments", "schema": {"description": "List Claude Code Remote environments for the current user. Returns environment IDs, names, kinds, and states. Use this to pick an environment_id for create_session.", "eager_input_streaming": true, "input_schema": {"properties": {"limit": {"description": "Maximum number of environments to return (default 20, max 100).", "type": "integer"}}, "required": [], "type": "object"}, "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__list_environments"}, "server": "claude-code-remote"}, {"description": "List transcript events for a Claude Code Remote session (user messages, assistant responses, tool calls, and system events, including model_fallback / model_refusal_fallback notices, which name original_model and fallback_model when the CLI reports them) so you can see what another session is working on. A call with no cursor returns the newest events, in the order they happened; has_more then means older events remain, and before_id set to first_id reads the page before these. A transcript is mostly hook, stream and progress events; to answer a narrow question (what was asked, what the session replied, how a turn ended) pass kinds so the page holds only those events.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__list_events", "schema": {"description": "List transcript events for a Claude Code Remote session (user messages, assistant responses, tool calls, and system events, including model_fallback / model_refusal_fallback notices, which name original_model and fallback_model when the CLI reports them) so you can see what another session is working on. A call with no cursor returns the newest events, in the order they happened; has_more then means older events remain, and before_id set to first_id reads the page before these. A transcript is mostly hook, stream and progress events; to answer a narrow question (what was asked, what the session replied, how a turn ended) pass kinds so the page holds only those events.", "eager_input_streaming": true, "input_schema": {"properties": {"after_id": {"description": "Pagination cursor: return the events just after this event ID. Pass the last_id of the page you hold to move forward, toward newer events.", "type": "string"}, "before_id": {"description": "Pagination cursor: return the events just before this event ID. Pass the first_id of the page you hold to go further back in the transcript.", "type": "string"}, "kinds": {"description": "Return only events of these kinds; omit for every kind. A kind is the key an event carries in data, e.g. [\"user\", \"assistant\", \"result\"] for the conversation without system (hook and init events, and notices such as model_fallback), env_manager_log, token deltas (stream_event) or tool_progress. An event with no such key, only internal_anthropic_catchall (its type field names it: permission_response, bash_command, session_notice, compaction and others), is kind \"other\". The filter runs after the page is read, so data can hold fewer events than limit, or none; first_id, last_id and has_more still describe the whole page read, so while has_more is true keep paging the same way (after a call with no cursor that is back, with before_id set to first_id).", "items": {"enum": ["env_manager_log", "control_response", "keep_alive", "system", "user", "assistant", "result", "control_request", "stream_event", "tool_progress", "tool_use_summary", "rate_limit_event", "other"], "type": "string"}, "type": "array"}, "limit": {"description": "Maximum number of events to read (default 20, max 100). With kinds, this counts events before the filter, so pass 100.", "type": "integer"}, "session_id": {"description": "The session ID to read events from.", "type": "string"}}, "required": ["session_id"], "type": "object"}, "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__list_events"}, "server": "claude-code-remote"}, {"description": "List repositories the current user has access to. Returns repo full_name (owner/repo), URL, and metadata such as visibility and last-push time. Use this to pick a repo for create_session sources, or to discover what's available before asking the user. Substring-filter with `query` (case-insensitive match against full_name) when looking for a specific repo.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__list_repos", "schema": {"description": "List repositories the current user has access to. Returns repo full_name (owner/repo), URL, and metadata such as visibility and last-push time. Use this to pick a repo for create_session sources, or to discover what's available before asking the user. Substring-filter with `query` (case-insensitive match against full_name) when looking for a specific repo.", "eager_input_streaming": true, "input_schema": {"properties": {"limit": {"description": "Maximum number of repos to return (default 50, max 200). Applied after the query filter.", "type": "integer"}, "query": {"description": "Optional case-insensitive substring matched against full_name (owner/repo). Empty matches everything.", "type": "string"}}, "required": [], "type": "object"}, "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__list_repos"}, "server": "claude-code-remote"}, {"description": "List Claude Code Remote sessions visible to the authenticated account. In bot contexts (e.g. Slack) this is a shared pool spanning many people, not just the human asking — pass mine: true to narrow to sessions started by the same account as the calling session. Returns session IDs, titles, statuses, and timestamps. status_bucket is the board bucket (working / blocked / review_ready / completed / failed) — read this for \"running / waiting on you / done / errored\"; updated_at is the last event. Where shown, a row also has parent_session_id, task_summary and post_turn_summary (recent_action, needs_action). To read how a session's last turn ended, call list_events with kinds [\"result\"] and limit 100.", "name": "mcp__claude-code-remote__list_sessions", "schema": {"description": "List Claude Code Remote sessions visible to the authenticated account. In bot contexts (e.g. Slack) this is a shared pool spanning many people, not just the human asking — pass mine: true to narrow to sessions started by the same account as the calling session. Returns session IDs, titles, statuses, and timestamps. status_bucket is the board bucket (working / blocked / review_ready / completed / failed) — read this for \"running / waiting on you / done / errored\"; updated_at is the last event. Where shown, a row also has parent_session_id, task_summary and post_turn_summary (recent_action, needs_action). To read how a session's last turn ended, call list_events with kinds [\"result\"] and limit 100.", "eager_input_streaming": true, "input_schema": {"properties": {"after_id": {"description": "Pagination cursor: return sessions older than this session ID. Pass the last_id from a previous response to get the next page.", "type": "string"}, "before_id": {"description": "Pagination cursor: return sessions newer than this session ID. Pass the first_id from a previous response to get the previous page.", "type": "string"}, "children": {"description": "Set true to narrow the list to the sessions that this session created with create_session. Their rows 